# A comparison that CPython refuses answers in the compiled backend. `xs[0] > "a"` is an ordering
# between a number and a text — Python raises TypeError naming both kinds, the interpreter raises
# it through the same gate, and the compiled path folds the container read to the literal element
# and then folds the *condition* rather than trapping, so the ternary takes the true branch and
# prints 1. Both spellings are pinned: the int one is the shape that predates this cycle, the bool
# one is what made it visible (roadmap Gap R.119, measured while closing Gap R.112).
xs = [1]
print(1 if xs[0] > "a" else 0)
ys = [True]
print(1 if ys[0] > "a" else 0)
